There are several methods for copying pirated books. The most common is via desktop printing, where copied books are printed on standard sheet-fed printers or photocopiers in paperback form; this can create low-cost, low-quality copies of a book which can be distributed quickly and widely. Less common is bookbinding, wherein hardcover copies of books are made by binding together blank paper; these look much like genuine hardcover books, but can be created very quickly and cheaply. Additionally, digital reproduction has become increasingly popular due to its ease - scanning a book and digitally reproducing it online allows for an infinite number of "copies" to be made at no cost.

These methods obviously have risks associated with them: the greatest risk being infringement of copyright laws as well as potential damage to the original work in question. Many countries also restrict pirated physical book publications via criminal law or market regulation - meaning those involved in the manufacture or sale face charges ranging from civil lawsuits to jail time.
